      "source_number": "497",
      "mathematical_status": "determinate",
      "independent_solution": "The principal square root makes every a_n nonnegative, and n must be a positive integer. For 6, n^2+5n=36 gives (n-4)(n+9)=0; n=4 is valid and a_4=6. For -6, nonnegativity excludes membership immediately; squaring would spuriously reproduce n=4 but a_4 is +6. For 5, n^2+5n=25 has roots (-5+/-5sqrt(5))/2, neither a positive integer. Equivalently, n^2+5n increases strictly for n>=1 because its next-term increment is 2n+6>0, and at n=3 it is 24 while at n=4 it is 36; no integer index can produce 25. Thus exactly 6, not -6 or 5, is a term.",
